  "result": "Fixed. `python3 -c \"from shop.cli import main; main(['quote','a:12.5:2'])\"` now prints **27.06**. It printed 26.09 before.\n\n**Root cause:** in `shop/money.py`, `to_cents` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")` turned `\"12.5\"` into 12 dollars and `\"05\"`, giving 1205 cents instead of 1250. I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. I also removed the `or 0`, which wasn't needed because `\"\".ljust(2, \"0\")` is `\"00\"`.\n\nWith the fix, the numbers work out: 1250 \u00d7 2 = 2500 cents, plus 8.25% tax (206.25, rounded to 206) gives 2706, which prints as 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. All 6 pass. None of them test `to_cents` directly, which is how this bug got through.\n\n**Something I didn't fix:** `to_cents` also gets negative amounts wrong. `\"-1.50\"` becomes `-100 + 50 = -50` instead of `-150`. The quote command doesn't hit this, but anything else that parses negative amounts would.",
